Flat LikeOS title bars (ModernTitles) and anti-aliased Xft fonts.

Additions, all switched on from the configuration and inert otherwise.

1. "xft:<fontconfig pattern>" as a font name (TitleFont, MenuFont, ...).
   ctwm draws every string through XmbTextExtents/XmbDrawString on a MyFont's
   fontset.  ctwm.h redirects those two calls to wrappers in util.c, which
   send a fontset that stands for an Xft font to Xft (UTF-8; the colour comes
   from the GC's foreground) and everything else to Xlib unchanged.  The
   fontset is still created, from "fixed", so code that only knows fontsets
   keeps a valid one; the MyFont's metrics are the Xft font's.  cursor.c
   opts out: string cursors are drawn into a one-bit bitmap.

2. ModernTitles, with TitleBarHeight and TitleButtonWidth: the LikeOS title
   bar (win_modern.c).  A flat band, #eef2f9 with near-black text when the
   window has the focus and #f5f7fb with slate text when not; the
   application's icon (_NET_WM_ICON at 16 pixels) 12 pixels in, and its
   title, ellipsised, after it; on the right the caption buttons, each
   TitleButtonWidth wide, TitlePadding apart and FramePadding from the edge,
   drawn as a rounded rectangle 26 pixels tall inset in the band (the whole
   height of the band takes the click).  Each carries a small line glyph,
   10x10 pixels with a 1.25-pixel stroke and round ends, chosen by the
   button's function -- a line for f.iconify, a rounded square (two offset
   ones while zoomed) for the zoom functions, a cross for f.delete and
   f.destroy.  At rest the button is only its glyph; under the pointer the
   rectangle fills with a faint blue-grey wash, the close button with coral
   red and a white cross.  Buttons bound to anything else keep their images.
   The title window's bottom border, the only part of it the frame does not
   clip, takes the title colour, so no line separates the caption from the
   window contents.

   Menus get rows 30 pixels tall with the text 12 pixels in, the entry under
   the pointer as a rounded rectangle inset 4 pixels in its row, a hairline
   border in MenuShadowColor (a light blue-grey in the LikeOS configuration,
   which turns the drop shadow itself off) rather than in the text colour,
   and separators in a lighter shade of it, 10 pixels short of the edges.

   Glyphs, buttons, highlights and icons are rendered client-side into
   XImages, blended against the known background colour; TrueColor visuals
   only (anything else keeps the classic decorations).

3. With ModernTitles, a resize border: the band a few pixels OUTSIDE a
   window's one-pixel edge, invisible, where the pointer turns into a
   double arrow (diagonal at the corners) and a drag resizes the window
   from that edge or corner (win_modern.c, "The resize border").
   It is an InputOnly window 6 pixels larger than the frame on every side,
   stacked directly beneath it, so the frame covers its middle and every
   window above the frame covers it too.  Not for maximised windows,
   windows of fixed size, borderless windows or ctwm's own.  A press on it
   (or on the frame's own edge) has the frame's bindings, raises and focuses
   the window, and StartResize() moves exactly the edges under the pointer,
   keeping their distance from it.  To follow its frame through every move,
   resize, restack, map, unmap and reparent -- some thirty places in ctwm --
   ctwm.h routes those Xlib calls through wrappers in win_modern.c, which
   bring the border along when the window is a frame that has one.
   Before a frame is restacked its border is lowered out of the way: ctwm
   raises a window by placing it directly above the next frame down, which
   would otherwise put it under its own border for a moment -- the
   application was told the pointer left and came back on every click
   (the wheel included), and GDK, taking that as a new scroll gesture,
   dropped the smooth-scrolling position: the wheel did nothing.

4. With ModernTitles, a maximised window stays maximised until the user
   restores it: a client's request to move or resize a zoomed window is
   answered with its current geometry instead of being carried out (which
   also unzoomed it) -- Claws Mail sized its window from saved settings at
   start and shrank the window the user had just maximised; and restoring a
   window no longer warps the pointer into it (it jumped to the window's
   left edge after the restore button had shrunk the window away from under
   it).
   A double click on a caption maximises the window or restores it (same
   window, within ConstrainedMoveTime and 4 pixels of the first press);
   ctwm's constrained move, which a double click started and which warped
   the pointer to the middle of the window, is off in this mode.

Hooks: the focus change repaints the caption (win_ops.c, after
hasfocusvisible is set -- and not the buttons before AddWindow has created
them); Enter/Leave on a caption button set its hover state (event_handlers.c;
the buttons now select those events); SetupFrame repaints the buttons, since
zooming moves them by gravity without an Expose; a _NET_WM_ICON change drops
the cached title icon (ewmh.c); window teardown frees it and destroys the
resize border; AddWindow creates the border once the frame's contexts are
saved; a press on the border is a frame press (event_handlers.c), motion over
it picks its cursor.

